{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 58, :comments ["<b>And the garment, or the warp, or the woof, or any article of skin, which you shall wash.</b> Scripture stated in the verses above that a garment in which the plague reappeared after washing is fit to be torn from the garment, and if it is seen further spreading there, it is 'flecked' and fit to be burned; and if the plague departs from the garment upon washing, he is obligated to immerse it and then it shall be pure — this is what it says, <b>and it shall be washed a second time and be clean.</b> <br>And know that the plagues were a sign and wonder upon sin, for so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: to what may the matter be compared? To an evil servant who was being sold. When his master came to buy him, he knew that he was an evil servant, so he took chains with him, so that if he rebelled he would subdue him with them. Once he rebelled, he brought those chains and bound him. So too, the Holy One, blessed be He — it was revealed and known before Him that man was destined to sin, and He prepared these afflictions, so that if he sinned He would subdue him with them. And so the plagues would come: the stones would be stricken first, as it is written (Leviticus 14:34), ״ונתתי נגע צרעת בבית ארץ אחוזתכם״ — if he repents, well; and if not, He strikes him in his garments, as it says (Leviticus 13:47), ״והבגד כי יהיה בו נגע צרעת״ — if he repents, well; and if not, He strikes him in his body. And how in his body? Little by little: it begins with the baldness of his head, as it says (Leviticus 13:40), ״ואיש כי ימרט ראשו״ — and even in the baldness of his head there is still a doubt: perhaps impure, perhaps pure — if he repents, well; and if not, He strikes him with a boil, as it says, ״אדם כי יהיה בעורו שחין״ — if he repents, well; and if not, He strikes him with severe plagues: שאת וספחת ובהרת. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in the Midrash of Ruth: the Merciful One does not exact punishment from persons first. From whom do you learn it? From Job — he was stricken first in his property, and afterward in his sons and the members of his household, and afterward in his body. And likewise in Egypt: first in their property, as it says (Psalms 105:33), ״ויך גפנם ותאנתם ויסגר לברד בעירם״, and afterward (Psalms 78:51), ״ויך כל בכור במצרים״. And so too with plagues: first they come upon his house — if he repents, they require only the removal of the stones, as it says (Leviticus 14:40), ״וחלצו את האבנים אשר בהן הנגע״; and if not, they require demolition, as it says (Leviticus 14:45), ״ונתץ את הבית את אבניו ואת עציו״. And afterward they come upon his garments — if he repents, well; and if not, they require tearing, as it says (Leviticus 13:56), ״וקרע את הבגד״; and if not, they require burning, as it says (Leviticus 13:55), ״ושרף את הבגד״. And afterward they come upon his body — if he repents, well; and if not (Leviticus 13:46), ״בדד ישב מחוץ למחנה מושבו״. Thus far in the Midrash." "<b>And behold</b> the plagues were an indication of absolute providence and were not a natural phenomenon but a great miracle, and the matter did not occur outside the Land but only in the Land of Israel. For when one of Israel committed a sin and transgression, the plague would appear in his house or in his garment or on his body, to testify upon him that there is sin in his hand, as it is written (Psalms 89:33), ״ופקדתי בשבט פשעם ובנגעים עונם״ — and so that he might repent and be purified and cleansed of his iniquity. And this is what is also alluded to regarding this (Leviticus 13:13), ״כלו הפך לבן טהור הוא״ — that is, pure of the plague and of the iniquity. And concerning this Scripture says (Leviticus 14:34), ״ונתתי נגע צרעת בבית ארץ אחוזתכם״, and this is what is further written there, ״כי תבאו אל ארץ כנען אשר אני נותן לכם לאחוזה״ — to indicate that the matter applies only in the inheritance of HaShem: not in the land of the nations, which is profane, and not in Jerusalem, which is the House of HaShem uniquely designated for His dwelling and was not apportioned to the tribes, but in the land called the inheritance of HaShem, which was apportioned to the tribes — it is the holy and chosen Land called the land of possession. Happy is he who merits to see it in its rebuilding. May the Holy One, blessed be He, merit us to be among those who dwell in its shade, for it corresponds to the Temple on high, concerning which David, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 65:5), ״אשרי תבחר ותקרב ישכון חצריך נשבעה בטוב ביתך קדוש היכלך״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The verse quoted as שם י״ג (Leviticus 13) for והבגד כי יהיה בו נגע צרעת is Leviticus 13:47, not 13; likewise several other verse references within the chain of prooftexts are imprecise in the original but have been normalized to their correct Leviticus and Psalms citations. The phrase והבגד כי יהיה בו נגע צרעת appears in the Hebrew text as נצע rather than נגע, apparently a typographical error.", 1 "The Psalm citation (תהילים פט) is Psalms 89; the verse partially quoted is 89:33, normalized. The word הנה in the lemma appears to open a continuation from the preceding comment rather than corresponding to a specific word in Leviticus 13:58."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:36.502055548Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}